# =============================================================================
#  .dockerignore — what never reaches the build context
# =============================================================================
#
#  Two jobs. The first is speed: everything listed here is excluded before the
#  daemon receives the context, so a build does not begin by uploading a
#  200MB node_modules it is about to throw away.
#
#  The second is safety, and it is the reason this file is not optional.
#  `COPY src ./src` copies what the DAEMON has, not what you think you asked
#  for — so anything sitting in this directory is a candidate for the image
#  unless it is named here.
# =============================================================================

# ── Secrets ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# This is the line that separates a shareable image from a credential leak.
#
# .env holds MONGO_URI (with its password), JWT_SECRET, ADMIN_PASSWORD and
# SHOPIFY_PARTNER_API_TOKEN — read access to your entire Partner organisation.
# Nothing in the Dockerfile COPYs it by name, but that is not protection: a
# broad `COPY . .` in some future edit would sweep it in, and once a file is in
# a layer it is in that layer FOREVER. Deleting it in a later RUN does not
# remove it — the layer still ships, and `docker history`, a `docker save`
# tarball, or anyone who pulls the image can read it straight out.
#
# Pushing such an image to a registry is disclosing those credentials to
# everyone who can pull it, and there is no way to un-ring that bell short of
# rotating every secret in the file.
#
# Configuration reaches the container at RUN time instead: --env-file .env,
# compose's `environment:`, or your orchestrator's secret store. dotenv never
# overwrites an already-set variable, so that always wins.
#
# .env.example is excluded by the same pattern, deliberately. It is a template
# for a human reading the repository, the image has no use for it, and "no
# .env-shaped file ever enters the build context" is a rule worth keeping
# without exceptions.
.env
.env.*
*.pem
*.key

# ── Dependencies ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Rebuilt inside the image by `npm ci`. Copying the host's tree in would be
# actively harmful, not merely wasteful: it is installed for the host platform
# (macOS/arm64 on this machine) and the image is Alpine/musl. Any package with a
# native binding would fail to load at runtime with an error that names the
# architecture, not the cause.
node_modules

# ── Build output ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The image compiles its own dist/ in the build stage. A stale host dist/ copied
# in could leave orphaned .js files from sources that have since been deleted or
# renamed — and Node resolves whatever is on disk, so the image would run code
# that no longer exists in src/. Same hazard the .gitignore comment describes.
dist
*.tsbuildinfo

# ── Version control ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Large, and it carries full history — including any secret that was ever
# committed and later removed, which is still readable in the object store.
.git
.gitignore
.gitattributes

# ── Tests ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# tsconfig already excludes test/ from compilation, so this changes nothing
# about the output — it only keeps the context small and the runtime image
# honest about what it contains. Run the suite in CI, before the build.
test
coverage

# ── Documentation ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Read on GitHub, never by the process.
docs
*.md

# ── Tooling and editor / OS noise ───────────────────────────────────────────
# Linting is a CI concern; the compiler in stage 2 does not consult it.
eslint.config.js
.editorconfig
.vscode
.idea
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# ── Logs and local runtime state ────────────────────────────────────────────
*.log
logs
npm-debug.log*

# ── Docker's own files ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Nothing inside the image reads these. Excluding them also means editing a
# comment in the Dockerfile does not invalidate a COPY layer.
Dockerfile
Dockerfile.*
.dockerignore
docker-compose*.yml

# ── Credential files on disk ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The header above explains why this file is not optional. These are the
# on-disk counterpart of .env: `.env.example` invites the operator to point
# GOOGLE_APPLICATION_CREDENTIALS at a service-account key, and a key swept into
# a layer is in that layer forever — `docker history` and `docker save` both
# read it back out.
secret/
secrets/
*.pem
*.key
*.p12
*.pfx
*-key.json
*service-account*.json
*credentials*.json
